**Time Complexity:**

| Operation                 | Complexity    |
| ------------------------- | ------------- |
| Generate all substrings   | O(n²)         |
| Build substring frequency | O(n)          |
| Build target frequency    | O(m)          |
| Compare frequencies       | O(256) = O(1) |

**Total Time Complexity:** O(n³ + n²m)

**Space Complexity:** O(256) = O(1), excluding the returned substring.

**Note:** This approach is extremely slow for large inputs because it generates every possible substring and recalculates its frequency from scratch.

class Solution {
public:
    string minWindow(string s, string t) {
        int n = s.size();
        int m = t.size();

        int ansLen = INT_MAX;
        string ans = "";

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {

                unordered_map<char, int> freq;

                for (int k = i; k <= j; k++) {
                    freq[s[k]]++;
                }

                unordered_map<char, int> need;

                for (char c : t) {
                    need[c]++;
                }

                bool valid = true;

                for (auto it : need) {
                    if (freq[it.first] < it.second) {
                        valid = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }

                if (valid && j - i + 1 < ansLen) {
                    ansLen = j - i + 1;
                    ans = s.substr(i, ansLen);
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};

Better Approach – Prefix Frequency + Substring Checking
We can improve the brute-force approach by avoiding frequency calculation from scratch for every substring.
Approach
Instead of recalculating character frequencies for every substring:
1. Build a prefix frequency array for s.
2. Generate all possible substrings.
3. Calculate the frequency of each character in substring [i, j] using:


### **Complexity Analysis**

| Operation                                     | Complexity |
| --------------------------------------------- | ---------- |
| Build prefix frequency arrays                 | O(128n)    |
| Generate all substrings                       | O(n²)      |
| Check each substring using prefix frequencies | O(128)     |

**Time Complexity:**

O(128n + 128n²)

Since 128 is a constant:

**O(n²)**

**Space Complexity:**

O(128n) = **O(n)**

We store prefix frequency arrays for all positions in the string.

**Note:** This approach is significantly better than the previous brute-force approach because we avoid recalculating substring frequencies. However, O(n²) is still too slow for n = 10⁵. We need an O(n) Sliding Window approach to solve this efficiently.


class Solution {
public:
    string minWindow(string s, string t) {
        int n = s.size();

        vector<vector<int>> pref(n + 1, vector<int>(128, 0));

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            pref[i + 1] = pref[i];
            pref[i + 1][(unsigned char)s[i]]++;
        }

        vector<int> need(128, 0);

        for (char c : t) {
            need[(unsigned char)c]++;
        }

        int minLen = INT_MAX;
        int start = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {

                bool valid = true;

                for (int c = 0; c < 128; c++) {
                    int freq = pref[j + 1][c] - pref[i][c];

                    if (freq < need[c]) {
                        valid = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }

                if (valid && j - i + 1 < minLen) {
                    minLen = j - i + 1;
                    start = i;
                }
            }
        }

        if (start == -1) return "";

        return s.substr(start, minLen);
    }
};

Optimal Approach – Sliding Window + Two Pointers
Core intuition
Instead of generating every substring, maintain a window using two pointers:
- left – starting index of the current window.
- right – ending index of the current window.
We expand the window until it contains all required characters. Once it becomes valid, we shrink it from the left to find the smallest possible window.

### **Complexity Analysis**

**Time Complexity:**

| Operation                      | Complexity |
| ------------------------------ | ---------- |
| Build target frequency array   | O(m)       |
| Move right pointer through `s` | O(n)       |
| Move left pointer through `s`  | O(n)       |

Since both pointers traverse the string at most once:

\\[ O(m+n+n) = O(n+m) \\]

**Time Complexity: O(n + m)**

**Space Complexity:**

We use a fixed-size frequency array of 128 characters.

\\[ O(128) = O(1) \\]

**Space Complexity: O(1)**

**Conclusion:** This solution uses the Sliding Window + Two Pointers technique and achieves linear time complexity, satisfying LeetCode 76's follow-up requirement.


class Solution {
public:
    string minWindow(string s, string t) {

        int n = s.size();

        vector<int> need(128, 0);

        for (char c : t) {
            need[(unsigned char)c]++;
        }

        int left = 0;
        int missing = t.size();

        int minLen = INT_MAX;
        int start = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {

            char c = s[right];

            if (need[(unsigned char)c] > 0) {
                missing--;
            }

            need[(unsigned char)c]--;

            while (missing == 0) {

                if (right - left + 1 < minLen) {
                    minLen = right - left + 1;
                    start = left;
                }

                char d = s[left];

                need[(unsigned char)d]++;

                if (need[(unsigned char)d] > 0) {
                    missing++;
                }

                left++;
            }
        }

        if (minLen == INT_MAX) {
            return "";
        }

        return s.substr(start, minLen);
    }
};
